  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nAs the upper trough deepens and pushes east out of the region, it\ncontinues to push a cooling airmass southeastward. We'll be seeing\nmuchbelow average temperatures this afternoon and tonight with \nlows in the 40s. An isolated dip into the upper 30s will not be \nout of the questions, depending on cloud cover. Passing rain \nshowers will continue through the evening as they move east out of\nthe region. Accumulation will overall be light, most locations \nseeing not much more than a few hundredths, but could be up to \n0.10\" in some places seeing steadier rainfall and better upper \nlevel support.\n\nLonger term, ensembles fully exit the trough to the east by \nWednesday allowing a new ridge to establish over the Pacific \nNorthwest. This will slowly extend increasing height rises and\nlowering RH values over eastern Montana with northwest flow \naloft, bringing a steady increase in temps. Highs Thursday will \nreach up to low 90s and look to last into the beginning of the \nnext week. Friday looks to be the hottest day at this time, but\nfurther model runs have been bringing warmer temperatures earlier\ntowards midweek. The current ridge crest looks like it may pass \nthe region around Sunday, which could introduce increased chances \nfor rain thereafter. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nDeviations from NBM included ARW and SREF blend for PoPs through \nthis evening. Increased winds for a few hours this afternoon\nfollowing obs. Used HRRRSmoke data in for Vis, mostly to remove\nerroneous NBM/GLAMP lowered visibilities.",
